    28	---
    29	
    30	## Statement
    31	
    32	**FALSE.** Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Cardinal
    33	exponentiation is strictly monotone in the base: for all cardinals
    34	$\kappa, \lambda, \mu$ ([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]),
    35	
    36	$$\kappa < \lambda \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \kappa^{\mu} < \lambda^{\mu} .$$
    37	
    38	The claim is plausible because the **weak** form is true —
    39	$\kappa \le \lambda$ does give $\kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$
    40	([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]) — and because Cantor's theorem supplies
    41	the different strict inequality $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$ at every cardinal
    42	([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]]). It fails already at
    43	$\kappa = \aleph_0$, $\lambda = \aleph_1$, $\mu = \aleph_0$, where both powers
    44	collapse to $2^{\aleph_0}$.
    45	
    46	## Facts & Assumptions
    47	
    48	**Given:** The Axiom of Choice, so that every exponential written here is defined ([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
    49	
    50	[L1] $\kappa \le \lambda$ implies $\kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$; and $(\mu^{\nu})^{\rho} = \mu^{\nu \otimes \rho}$ ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
    51	
    52	[L2] $\kappa \otimes \kappa = \kappa$ for every infinite cardinal $\kappa$ ([[thm-hessenberg]]).
    53	
    54	[L3] $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$ and $2^{\kappa}$ is a cardinal ([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]]).
    55	
    56	[L4] $\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above $\aleph_0$, and $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]], [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]]).
    57	
    58	[L5] $\aleph_0 = \omega$ is an infinite cardinal and $2 \in \omega$ is a cardinal with $2 \le \aleph_0$ ([[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
    59	
    60	[L6] Ordinals satisfy trichotomy and $\alpha \notin \alpha$ ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]]).
    61	
    62	## Refutation
    63	
    64	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
    65	
    66	1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $\kappa < \lambda$ implies $\kappa^{\mu} < \lambda^{\mu}$ for all cardinals. [assume-contra]
    67	
    68	1.2 By [L3] the cardinal $2^{\aleph_0}$ satisfies $\aleph_0 < 2^{\aleph_0}$, so [L4] gives $\aleph_1 \le 2^{\aleph_0}$; with [L5] this chains to $2 \le \aleph_0 < \aleph_1 \le 2^{\aleph_0}$. [L3, L4, L5]
    69	
    70	2.1 Applying [L1] along that chain, $2^{\aleph_0} \le \aleph_0^{\aleph_0} \le \aleph_1^{\aleph_0} \le (2^{\aleph_0})^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0 \otimes \aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0}$, the last two equalities by [L1] and [L2]; so all four values are equal and in particular $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1^{\aleph_0}$. [step 1.2, L1, L2, L5, L6]
    71	
    72	3.1 But $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$ by step 1.2, so the assumed claim at $\kappa = \aleph_0$, $\lambda = \aleph_1$, $\mu = \aleph_0$ gives $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} < \aleph_1^{\aleph_0}$, contradicting step 2.1 by [L6]; therefore exponentiation is not strictly monotone in the base. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, L6, discharge-contradiction] ∎
    73	
    74	## Remarks
    75	
    76	**Why the collapse happens.** For an infinite exponent $\mu$: once the base is at least $2$ and at most $2^{\mu}$, raising it to the power $\mu$ gives the same value, because $(2^{\mu})^{\mu} = 2^{\mu \otimes \mu} = 2^{\mu}$ squeezes the chain shut. So for infinite $\mu$ strict monotonicity in the base can only survive where the base is allowed to exceed $2^{\mu}$, and it fails on the whole interval below it; for finite exponents $\ge 1$ the collapse does not occur, which is why the witness above takes $\mu = \aleph_0$. That is a fact about [[thm-hessenberg]] as much as about exponentiation.
    77	
    78	**The weak form is not damaged.** $\kappa \le \lambda \Rightarrow \kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$ remains true, and so does Cantor's strict inequality $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$. What fails is the strict form in the base, and it fails at the smallest infinite instance.
    79	
    80	**A second casualty of the same computation.** The chain in step 2.1 also shows $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0}$, so raising $\aleph_0$ to its own power adds nothing beyond taking the power set of $\omega$. The companion page carries that computation on its own, together with the corresponding value for $\lvert \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}} \rvert$.
