    38	---
    39	
    40	## Statement
    41	
    42	Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. For a set $A$ let $\aleph(A)$ be its
    43	Hartogs number ([[thm-hartogs]]), the least ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]) admitting
    44	no injection into $A$. Then:
    45	
    46	**(a)** $\aleph(A)$ is a cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]), for **every** set $A$,
    47	well-orderable or not.
    48	
    49	**(b)** If $\kappa$ is a cardinal then $\kappa < \aleph(\kappa)$, and every
    50	cardinal $\lambda$ with $\kappa < \lambda$ satisfies $\aleph(\kappa) \le \lambda$.
    51	So $\aleph(\kappa)$ is the **least cardinal strictly above $\kappa$**.
    52	
    53	**(c)** If $\kappa$ is an infinite cardinal then so is $\aleph(\kappa)$.
    54	
    55	**What this supplies, and what it does not.** It gives a successor operation on
    56	cardinals in ZF alone: there is always a next one, and it is definable. It says
    57	nothing about how *large* the next cardinal is compared with $2^{\kappa}$; that
    58	comparison is not decided by the axioms in use here.
    59	
    60	## Facts & Assumptions
    61	
    62	**Given:** A set $A$ and a cardinal $\kappa$, in ZF, with no choice principle.
    63	
    64	[L1] $\aleph(A)$ is an ordinal that does not inject into $A$, and it is the least such; consequently every ordinal $\beta \in \aleph(A)$ does inject into $A$ ([[thm-hartogs]]).
    65	
    66	[L2] An ordinal $\kappa$ is a cardinal when no $\alpha \in \kappa$ satisfies $\alpha \approx \kappa$ ([[def-cardinal]], [[def-equinumerous]]).
    67	
    68	[L3] For cardinals, $\kappa \le \lambda$ if and only if there is an injection $\kappa \to \lambda$ (claim (a) of [[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
    69	
    70	[L4] Ordinals satisfy trichotomy, $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$, and every element of an ordinal is an ordinal ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]]).
    71	
    72	[L5] A composition of an injection with a bijection is an injection, a subset inclusion is an injection, and the identity is a bijection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
    73	
    74	[L6] For a well-orderable $X$, $\lvert X\rvert = X$ when $X$ is a cardinal, and $\lvert \alpha\rvert \le \alpha$ ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
    75	
    76	## Proof
    77	
    78	**Proof technique:** direct.
    79	
    80	1.1 Every $\beta \in \aleph(A)$ injects into $A$, and $\aleph(A)$ itself does not. [L1]
    81	
    82	1.2 If $\lambda$ is a cardinal with $\kappa < \lambda$ then $\lambda$ does not inject into $\kappa$: an injection $\lambda \to \kappa$ would give $\lambda \le \kappa$ by [L3], contradicting $\kappa \in \lambda$ and trichotomy. [L3, L4]
    83	
    84	2.1 Claim (a): if $\beta \in \aleph(A)$ had $\beta \approx \aleph(A)$, then composing a bijection $\aleph(A) \to \beta$ with an injection $\beta \to A$ supplied by step 1.1 would inject $\aleph(A)$ into $A$, which step 1.1 forbids; so no element of $\aleph(A)$ is equinumerous with it and $\aleph(A)$ is a cardinal by [L2]. [step 1.1, L2, L5]
    85	
    86	2.2 First half of claim (b): $\kappa$ injects into $\kappa$ by the identity, so $\kappa \ne \aleph(\kappa)$ by step 1.1; and $\aleph(\kappa) \in \kappa$ is impossible, since then $\aleph(\kappa) \subseteq \kappa$ by [L4] and the inclusion would inject it into $\kappa$; trichotomy leaves $\kappa \in \aleph(\kappa)$, that is $\kappa < \aleph(\kappa)$. [step 1.1, L4, L5]
    87	
    88	3.1 Second half of claim (b) and claim (c): a cardinal $\lambda$ with $\kappa < \lambda$ does not inject into $\kappa$ by step 1.2, so $\aleph(\kappa) \le \lambda$ by the minimality in [L1]; with step 2.2 and step 2.1 this makes $\aleph(\kappa)$ the least cardinal strictly above $\kappa$; and if $\omega \le \kappa$ then $\omega \le \kappa < \aleph(\kappa)$ by step 2.2, so $\aleph(\kappa)$ is infinite. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, L1, L4, L6] ∎
    89	
    90	## Remarks
    91	
    92	**Why claim (a) is stated for an arbitrary set.** For a cardinal $\kappa$ only the special case is needed here, but the general case costs the same two lines and is exactly what [[thm-tarski-square]] uses: there, $A$ is an arbitrary infinite set that is not known to be well-orderable, and the argument needs $\aleph(A)$ to be an infinite **cardinal** before Hessenberg's theorem can be applied to it.
    93	
    94	**No power set is involved.** [[thm-hartogs]] builds $\aleph(A)$ from the well-ordered subsets of $A$, so the successor cardinal is obtained without ever forming $\mathcal{P}(A)$ as a size. That separation is what makes the aleph hierarchy a ZF construction while the beth hierarchy is not.
    95	
    96	**The notation collides with the ordinal successor, and this page keeps them apart.** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is the **ordinal** successor ([[def-ordinal]]) and is almost never a cardinal: an infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, so $\kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$ is not one. The cardinal successor is $\aleph(\kappa)$, which is much larger. Where an item on this page writes the superscript on an ordinal letter, as [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] does with $\alpha^{+}$, it carries the published ordinal meaning $\alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$; the cardinal successor is always written $\aleph(\kappa)$ on this page.
