    39	---
    40	
    41	## Definition
    42	
    43	The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.
    44	
    45	> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function
    46	> ([[def-choice-function]]).
    47	
    48	Written out: for every set $\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,
    49	there exists a function $g$ with domain $\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \in S$ for
    50	all $S \in \mathcal{F}$.
    51	
    52	An equivalent formulation, immediate from the definition of the Cartesian
    53	product, is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if $X_i \ne \emptyset$
    54	for every $i \in I$, then $\prod_{i \in I} X_i \ne \emptyset$.
    55	
    56	## Remarks
    57	
    58	- **This is an axiom, not a theorem, and it is deliberately not derived here.**
    59	  **Assume ZF is consistent.** Then AC is independent of the remaining axioms of
    60	  Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory: Gödel (1938) showed that ZF, if consistent, cannot
    61	  refute it ([[rem-godel-constructible-universe]]), and Cohen (1963) showed that
    62	  ZF, if consistent, cannot prove it ([[rem-cohen-forcing-ac-independent]]). The
    63	  consistency hypothesis is not decoration and cannot be dropped: an inconsistent
    64	  ZF proves everything, AC included, so both halves of the independence would
    65	  fail. Nor can the hypothesis be discharged inside ZF. Both directions also
    66	  require machinery (the constructible universe and forcing) that this library
    67	  does not yet contain, so both are recorded with references rather than proved.
    68	  [[fs-zorn-provable-in-zf]] is the corresponding warning and carries the same
    69	  consistency assumption explicitly in its Given; [[rem-choice-strengths]]
    70	  locates the weaker choice principles.
    71	- Being an axiom, AC carries no well-definedness obligation, which is why this
    72	  item has no `justified_by`.
    73	- The case of a family listed by a natural number, which is the finite case once
    74	  finiteness is defined, is a theorem of ZF and needs no axiom
    75	  ([[lem-finite-choice]]). AC is exactly the extension of that theorem to
    76	  arbitrary index sets, and the gap between the two is not a matter of degree:
    77	  [[ex-russells-socks]] exhibits the difference concretely.
    78	- "ZFC" abbreviates ZF together with AC. Everything on this page after
    79	  [[lem-finite-choice]] that invokes AC is flagged as doing so, because later
    80	  pages need to state honestly which of their theorems are choice-free; that
    81	  bookkeeping is the purpose of [[rem-choice-strengths]].

