    29	
    30	## Statement
    31	
    32	**If ZFC together with "there is a proper class of strongly compact cardinals"
    33	is consistent, then so is ZF + "every uncountable cardinal is singular".**
    34	
    35	That is: relative to that large-cardinal hypothesis, there is a model of ZF in
    36	which no uncountable cardinal is regular at all, so $\aleph_1$, $\aleph_2$ and
    37	every other uncountable aleph has cofinality $\omega$. Gitik (1980) obtains it by
    38	an iterated Prikry-style forcing over a model with a proper class of strongly
    39	compact cardinals, followed by a symmetric submodel.
    40	
    41	## Remarks
    42	
    43	- **Not proved in this library.** No forcing, no large cardinals, and no
    44	  symmetric submodels: that part of the machinery is entirely outside the
    45	  library's stack. The cofinality *function* $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$ and the
    46	  words *regular* and *singular* are themselves defined here
    47	  ([[def-cofinality]]), and the library proves one regularity fact under choice
    48	  ([[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]]) — but that is choice-theoretic ground truth,
    49	  the opposite of what Gitik's result denies, and nothing here touches the
    50	  choice-free question this remark records.
    51	
    52	- **What would prove it.** Large-cardinal theory (strongly compact cardinals and
    53	  their embeddings), Prikry forcing and its iterations, and symmetric extensions.
    54	  This is the deepest result recorded on this page and is far outside the
    55	  library's stack.
    56	
    57	- **Why it matters here.** It is the extreme point of the phenomenon that
    58	  [[rem-feferman-levy-model]] introduces. That model makes $\omega_1$ singular;
    59	  Gitik's makes **every** uncountable cardinal singular. The practical reading
    60	  for this library is that regularity of an uncountable cardinal is never a free
    61	  structural fact about [[def-cardinal]]: it is a consequence of choice, and
    62	  without choice it can fail everywhere at once. What survives in ZF is the much
    63	  weaker existence statement [[thm-hartogs]], which is why the library leans on
    64	  Hartogs numbers, and why the one regularity statement it does prove — that no at
    65	  most countable subset of $\omega_1$ is cofinal in it — carries the Axiom of
    66	  Countable Choice as an explicit standing hypothesis rather than reading it off
    67	  the structure. [[rem-choice-ledger]] records that
    68	  distinction.
    69	
    70	- **Conditional discipline.** The hypothesis is a large-cardinal consistency
    71	  assumption strictly stronger than Con(ZFC), and the conclusion is a relative
    72	  consistency statement. Nothing here asserts that strongly compact cardinals
    73	  exist.

