    30	---
    31	
    32	## Statement
    33	
    34	**If ZF is consistent, then ZF is consistent with all of the following holding
    35	simultaneously:**
    36	
    37	- $\mathbb{R}$ is a countable union of countable sets;
    38	- the first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ is **singular**, indeed
    39	  $\mathrm{cf}(\omega_1) = \omega$;
    40	- consequently "a countable union of countable sets is countable" fails, and
    41	  $\omega_1$ is not regular.
    42	
    43	Feferman and Levy (1963) obtain this by collapsing: starting from a model of
    44	ZFC, force with the finite-support product that makes each $\aleph_n$ of the
    45	ground model countable, for $n \in \mathbb{N}$, and take the symmetric submodel
    46	with finite supports. The ground model's $\aleph_\omega$ becomes the new
    47	$\omega_1$, and it is the supremum of the countably many ordinals
    48	$\aleph_n$, each now countable, so its cofinality is $\omega$. The reals of the
    49	extension are the union over $n$ of the reals added at stage $n$, and each of
    50	those layers is countable in the extension.
    51	
    52	Note what does **not** fail: $\omega_1$ still exists, and $\mathbb{R}$ is still
    53	uncountable. A countable union of countable sets is being exhibited whose union
    54	is uncountable, which is possible exactly because no enumeration of the layers
    55	can be chosen uniformly.
    56	
    57	## Remarks
    58	
    59	- **Not proved in this library.** The Levy collapse and the symmetric submodel
    60	  are not developed here.
    61	
    62	- **What would prove it.** The forcing track of
    63	  [[rem-cohen-forcing-ac-independent]], specialised to the Levy collapse
    64	  $\mathrm{Coll}(\omega, {<}\aleph_\omega)$ with finite supports, plus the
    65	  cofinality computation in the symmetric model. Ordinal and cardinal arithmetic
    66	  beyond [[def-cardinal]] is also needed to state the cofinality claim properly.
    67	
    68	- **Why it matters here.** Two library results lean on it directly. It is the
    69	  external fact behind [[fs-countable-union-theorem-of-zf]]: the countable union
    70	  theorem [[thm-countable-union-of-countable]] is proved here **from**
    71	  [[def-countable-choice]], and this model shows the hypothesis cannot be
    72	  dropped. It is also the standing warning attached to $\omega_1$: any
    73	  counterexample built on the ordinal space $[0, \omega_1)$ and its sequential
    74	  compactness is silently spending a choice principle, because in this model
    75	  $\omega_1$ has countable cofinality and those arguments collapse. Note also
    76	  that [[thm-r-uncountable]] survives untouched: it is a theorem of ZF, and it is
    77	  not in tension with $\mathbb{R}$ being a countable union of countable sets.
    78	
    79	- **Conditional discipline.** Everything above is relative to the consistency of
    80	  ZF, and this library never asserts that the reals are a countable union of
    81	  countable sets, only that ZF alone cannot refute it.

